DTSTART:20261028T224500Z
DTEND:20261028T234500Z
DTSTAMP:20261028T000000Z
SUMMARY:That Thing: Workshop Series with Sarah Salvatoriello
DESCRIPTION:That Thing: A Self-Promotion Workshop Series\n\nA Workshop 
 Series over four Wednesdays in October for freelancers\, consultants\, and 
 small business owners (or\, really\, anyone who has ever gone blank when 
 someone asked what they do). Self-promotion isn't the 
 problem—inauthenticity is. Most creators don't hate marketing\;they hate 
 feeling like they're performing. These sessions challenge the expectations 
 for self-promotion and give you practical tools to embolden your efforts to 
 connect. Each night\nstands on its own and hands you something finished. 
 Take one or take all four\, and they build into a full picture of how your 
 brand should look and sound.\n\n \nAcross the series\, you will walk away 
 clear on: your values\, your one-liner\, a visual direction\, and a written 
 brand voice guide. No marketing background required. Bring the work you 
 already do and a willingness to name the part of it you usually leave 
 out.\n\nInstructed by Sarah Salvatoriello ( @sarahsalv)\, founder of 
 Ampersand & Ampersand (@ampersand.ampersand).  \n\nRegister separately for 
 each session you'd like to attend.\n\nSession Schedule\n\n\n	Oct 7 — How 
 to Self-Promote Best When Self-Promotion Is the Worst\n	Oct 14 — How to 
 Introduce Yourself\, on Purpose\n	Oct 21 — How to Make It Look Like 
 You\n	Oct 28 — How to Sound Like You (Even When a Robot's 
 Typing)\n\n\nOctober 7: How to Self-Promote Best When Self-Promotion Is the 
 Worst\nYou've built something impressive but keep describing it in the 
 safest possible terms. Name the values behind your work — and the one 
 thing about it you keep filing down\, which is probably your strongest 
 asset. Leave knowing what you stand for\, and why the vague version has 
 been costing you. \n\nOctober 14 — How to Introduce Yourself\,  on 
 Purpose\nEveryone has a version of the answer to “so  what do you do?” 
 that they wish they had giveninstead. Build the sentence you can say 
 without flinching\, out loud\, in the room\, tonight. You leave with a 
 one-liner that fits between the question and the elevator door. Bring a 
 rough\nidea of what you do — that's the whole prerequisite\n\nOctober 21 
 — How to Make It Look Like You\nYour work needs to look like it sounds. 
 Build a mood board that gives you a visual direction to point at — 
 colors\, type\, texture\, feeling — so every design choice you make 
 afterward has a foundation to build from. You leave with a mood board and 
 the vocabulary to explain it toa designer\, a printer\, or Claude at 1 am.  
  \n\nOctober 28 — How to Sound Like You (Even When a Robot's 
 Typing)\nWith so many tools for writing\, it’s hard to sound 
 authentically like yourself: your rhythms\, your words\, the phrases you 
 would never say. Come with a few things you have written — an email\, a 
 blog post\, a website page — and we work from what's already yours. Leave 
 with a brand voice guide you can use on your own or feed into AI\, so the 
 drafts that come back sound like a person\, not a robot. \n\nAbout the 
 Instructor\n\n Sarah Salvatoriello is the founder of Ampersand & 
 Ampersand\, a branding studio that helps untapped founders and brands align 
 their vision\, voice\, and visuals. She spent the first 18  years of her 
 career building in-house creative teams at global media companies like 
 Condé Nast\, Tripadvisor\, and The Knot. Sarah has many titles — 
 Creative Director\, Brand Strategist\, Armchair Psychologist\, Mom — and 
 each one supports her holistic approach to creative work\,  rooted in 
 finding the counterbalance no matter what she's working on. Her blend of 
 cheerful nihilism and compassionate-capitalist business empowerment swings 
 against toxic positivity without sacrificing effectiveness. She seeks out 
 the overlooked\, connects the objective to the subjective\, and advocates 
 for “real good” over “feel good.” \n\n
LOCATION:PGML: 3rd Floor | Conference Room 1
CATEGORIES:October, Popular Events, Popular Events > Educational Workshops
UID:LibCal-17419-17431441
URL:https://jclibrary.libcal.com/event/17431441
END:VEVENT
